.8% in Q1 2026, and Q2 was the first decline in three years.\n- Voltaris shipments fell 9% in Q2, the steepest drop among large makers.\n- Voltaris's share of Caldrian shipments fell from 31% to 27%. Tarnwell and imports gained it.\n- Shipments typically trail orders by about two quarters. A Q2 2026 decline therefore points to weak demand continuing into the second half of 2026 and early 2027.\n- Voltaris quarterly revenue (C$M): Q3 2025 905, Q4 2025 960, Q1 2026 980, Q2 2026 890.\n\n**Arithmetic**\n- The last four quarters sum to C$3,735M. Q2 annualised is C$3,560M.\n- Assuming about C$880M per quarter in the second half, 2026 revenue is about C$3.63B.\n- Reaching C$4.2B in 2027 needs about 16% growth over that base.\n- Industry shipments are flat to down. If volume is flat and share stays at 27%, revenue stays near C$3.6B.\n- Regaining the share lost, from 27% back to 31%, would add about 15% and reach roughly C$4.1B. Nothing in the share data suggests a rebound, because the trend is moving the other way.\n- Pricing could add a few percent. If share slips further to about 25%, revenue falls to roughly C$3.3B.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The data cover one quarter, so the trend could be noise.\n- Backlog, orders and price increases could close the gap, and those are in the scope of n2.\n- Shipments are in units and revenue also depends on mix and price, so the mapping is approximate.\n\n**Conclusion:** From the industry and share data alone, the C$4.2B guidance looks unlikely to be met.","ttft_ms":3414,"type":"result","duration_ms":20749,"uuid":"03a46441-7333-474c-af7e-c5f53724e4a0","ttft_stream_ms":2587,"time_to_request_ms":67,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":1,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":5,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":7,"engine_pickup":0,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":2,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements"